#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526161 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(4 x^{2} + 3 x y + y^{2} \\right)},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{8 x + 3 y}{4 x^{2} + 3 x y + y^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{3 x + 2 y}{4 x^{2} + 3 x y + y^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta} & =\\left(\\frac{3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} - \\frac{8 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}})+(\\frac{2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} + \\frac{3 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 6 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526166 :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} y - x y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=- 4 x y - y^{2},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =- 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta} & =(- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(r^{3} \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{3} \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =r^{3} \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{3} \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526174 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right)(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right)(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=- 2 x - y,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=- x + 4 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(4 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(4 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =4 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526176 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=- x^{2} y - x y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=- 2 x y - y^{2},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=- x^{2} - 2 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =- 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta} & =(- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(r^{3} \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r^{3} \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =r^{3} \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r^{3} \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526190 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=- 4 x y + 3 y^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 2 x^{2} + 6 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =6 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta} & =(3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(6 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- 3 r^{3} \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(6 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{3} \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =- 3 r^{3} \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r^{3} \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526192 :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=4 x^{2} - x y - 3 y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=8 x - y,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=- x - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =- 6 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta} & =(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 6 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 6 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 14 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526197 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} + 3 x y + 4 y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=- 4 x + 3 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3 x + 8 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =8 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(8 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(8 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =8 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526207 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 3 x y + y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=8 x + 3 y,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=3 x + 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta} & =(3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- 3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =- 3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 6 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526219 :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - x y - 4 y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\right)(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\right)(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=- 4 x - y,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=- x - 8 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =- 8 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 8 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(- 8 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =- 8 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526224 :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(4 x^{2} - 4 x y + 3 y^{2} \\right)},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{8 x - 4 y}{4 x^{2} - 4 x y + 3 y^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{- 4 x + 6 y}{4 x^{2} - 4 x y + 3 y^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{- 4 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{6 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta} & =\\left(\\frac{- 4 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right)(- r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+\\left(\\frac{6 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right)(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} - \\frac{8 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}})+(\\frac{6 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} - \\frac{4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526245 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{5}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{7}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le -\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{5}{3} & \\le -\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{5}{3}\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{25}{6} & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{25}{6}\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{6}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{7}{4} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{7}{4}\\le \\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{4} & \\le y\\le \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{21}{4}\\le y\\le \\frac{7}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526250 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{7}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} & \\le -\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} & \\le -\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\le \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{6} & \\le y\\le -\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{17}{6}\\le y\\le -\\frac{11}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526255 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{3}{4},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & \\le -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} & \\le -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{3}{4}\\le \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{7}{2}\\le \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{19}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{23}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{19}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{23}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526260 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{7}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{5}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & \\le -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{7}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{25}{12} & \\le x\\le \\frac{31}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{25}{12}\\le x\\le \\frac{31}{12}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{5}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{5}{3}\\le \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2\\le y\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526264 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{5}{2},\\,y=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{1}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & \\le -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{5}{2} & \\le -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{4} & \\le x\\le -\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{15}{4}\\le x\\le -\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{3} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{1}{3}\\le \\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{25}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{29}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{25}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{29}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526385 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta\\\\x^2&=(\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta)^2\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta\\\\y^2&=(\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta)^2\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=\\frac{121}{4}\\cos^2\\theta+\\frac{121}{4}\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}(1)\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526410 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + x y$ and $y=- \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x + y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 2 x - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 x - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}+\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 2 x - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526417 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - 2 x y$ and $y=- \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x - 2 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =4 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\  & =2 \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526421 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=8 x + y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =8 x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =8 x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 8 x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526423 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2 x e^{- 3 y}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=2 e^{- 3 y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 6 x e^{- 3 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2 e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 6 x e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}+\\left(- 6 x e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 24 x e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2 e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}} \\\\  & =- 2 \\left(12 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526426 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2 x e^{- 3 y}$ and $y=x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=2 e^{- 3 y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 6 x e^{- 3 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2 e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 6 x e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1x) & =1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}) & =- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(1x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 x \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}+\\left(- 6 x e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 4 x \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =24 x^{2} e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 6 x e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2 e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}} \\\\  & =2 \\left(12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 1\\right) e^{- 3 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526431 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + x y$ and $y=- x \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x + y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- x \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 2 x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1x) & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}) & =4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-1\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(-1x\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- x \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 2 x+\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 4 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 2 x \\\\  & =- 2 x \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526441 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y$ and $y=x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=8 x + y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 8 x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1x) & =1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}) & =2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(1x\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 8 x+\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 8 x \\\\  & =2 x \\left(x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526442 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- 3 x e^{4 y}$ and $y=x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 3 e^{4 y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 12 x e^{4 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 3 e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 12 x e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1x) & =1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}) & =- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(1x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 3 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}+\\left(- 12 x e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =36 x^{2} e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 12 x e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 3 e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\\\  & =3 \\left(12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526448 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - 2 x y$ and $y=- x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x - 2 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1x) & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}) & =2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-1\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(-1x\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =4 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x \\\\  & =2 x \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526455 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- 3 x e^{4 y}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 3 e^{4 y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 12 x e^{4 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 3 e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 12 x e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}+\\left(- 12 x e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 36 x e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 3 e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\\\  & =- 3 \\left(12 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} + 1\\right) e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527143 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527163 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527919 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{4},\\,y=8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{3},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=8\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527922 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{\\pi }\\right),\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\pi $ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi }\\right)=4\\pi (0)=0, \\\\ y & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{\\pi }\\right)+4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{\\pi }\\right)+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi }\\right)+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4\\cdot 0+4}=-\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,}=4\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-\\pi )$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-\\pi ) & =4(x-0) \\\\ y & =4x-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ is the $x$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527925 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5f5b235b54d2acd20297ab365d9b93907111b3fec9a86776c28e93fdfe105ee.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}3t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t)^{2}+(3t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2} \\\\  & =13{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}13{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =39{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527934 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527941 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-2,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope $m=-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+2) \\\\ y & =-x-2+1 \\\\ y & =-x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=-x-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527943 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t+1},\\,y={t}^{3}+4,\\,-1<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ y & =(1)^{3}+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},5).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}}\\right)} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =6\\cdot {1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\sqrt{2},5)$ and the slope $m=6\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527944 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1-{e}^{3t},\\,y=4{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-3{e}^{3(0)}=-3\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527946 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4t({t}^{2}+1)=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=3(0)^{2}-3=-3\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527965 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0<t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\n$$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{3(3)}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527971 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+8t,\\,y=4{t}^{7},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{2}+8(1)=2+8=10, \\\\ y & =4(1)^{7}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(10,4).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t+8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=28{t}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{28{t}^{6}}{4t+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{28\\cdot (1)^{6}}{4\\cdot (1)+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,4)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{7}{3}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527972 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line at $C$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the Cartesian equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =x+2 \\\\ y & =x+2+1 \\\\ y & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the normal line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527979 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t},\\,y=5{t}^{2}+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n10t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-{e}^{-0}=-1\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}]}
